--- Finding (zero_to_the_zero_power, a04): a fraction or a decimal of 1, 0 or -1 raised to an
--- int past 32 bits stops, where the int of the same value answers. Both modes agree:
--- run as it is, the program prints 1 (line 16), then stops at line 17 with
---   runtime error: line 17: 1 ^ 1099511627776: an exponent past 32 bits makes a number no memory holds
--- Line 18 alone stops the same way; line 19 alone stops with
---   runtime error: line 19: a whole number past 64 bits where an int is held in 64 bits: 1180591620717411303424
--- (and so do fraction[-1, 1] and decimal[1] to 2 ^ 70).
--- Expected: 1, 1 and 0 — the exact answers, as the int's power gives them (crow_runtime's
--- Int::pow answers 0, 1 and -1 to any power; Frac::pow and Dec::pow have no such case),
--- since kinds meet the exact way; at the least no message saying that 1 is a number no
--- memory holds. The second message does not name `^` as what took the int, where the spec
--- (Numbers That Keep Every Digit) says such a refusal names what took it ("a whole number
--- past 64 bits where range[] takes an int of 64 bits: …").
--- (Found while showing 0 ^ 0 in every kind of number: each kind answers 1 there.)

log[1 ^ (2 ^ 40)]
log[fraction[1, 1] ^ (2 ^ 40)]
log[decimal[1] ^ (2 ^ 40)]
log[fraction[0, 1] ^ (2 ^ 70)]
